e}}], "viewerUrl": "https://us.cloud.langfuse.com/project/cmmxymjnc032zad07j35swt3h/traces/97f5428647dde54061ab4d631330d86a", "ts": "2026-09-29T00:35:08.987Z"}, "id": 6, "domain": "VFD / Variable Frequency Drive Faults & Operation", "difficulty": "medium", "type": "scenario", "correct_answer": "A", "model_answer": "A", "parse_rule": "leading_letter", "is_correct": true, "http": 200, "turn_status": "answered", "basis": "general_reasoning", "trace_id": "97f5428647dde54061ab4d631330d86a", "response_time_ms": 4890, "answer_text": "A  \n\n- First, de‑energize the panel, lock‑out/tag‑out the VFD, and confirm no voltage is present before opening it.  \n- Check the VFD’s nameplate or manual for the ambient‑temperature derating curve; calculate the allowable current at 45 °C (typically a percentage reduction per degree above the rated 40 °C).  \n- Verify the drive’s actual load current (after power‑up) is below the derated limit; if it exceeds, reduce the load or improve cooling (add fans, increase ventilation, or relocate the panel).  \n- Inspect the panel for blocked airflow, dust, or failed cooling fans; clean or replace fans as needed.  \n- After corrective cooling/derating, restore power and monitor the drive temperature during operation.  \n\n(If you cannot locate the derating curve, consult the VFD’s full user manual for the exact factor.)"}
